# Button

Triggers an action on the current page — submitting a form, opening
an overlay, running an operation.

## Usage

`render_button` (`Gesso::Components::ButtonHelper`) validates the options and
renders the component; the block is the label.

```erb
<%%= render_button(variant: "primary") do %>
  Save changes
<%% end %>
```

`size` and `variant` combine freely, and any option below can be passed
in the same call:

```erb
<%%= render_button(variant: "destructive", size: "sm", type: "submit") do %>
  Delete record
<%% end %>
```

## When to use

- Performing an action: save, add, remove, open, confirm.
- Submitting a form (`type: "submit"`).

## When not to use

- Navigating to another page — use a link. If it changes the URL, it
  is a link, however button-like it looks.
- Switching views in place — use `tabs`.
- Toggling a setting — use `switch` or `checkbox`.

## Variants

<%= embed :button, :variants %>

| Variant | When to use |
| ------- | ----------- |
| `primary` | The view's single main action — Save, Submit, Confirm. Keep one per view. |
| `secondary` | A supporting action sitting beside the primary one. |
| `outline` | Secondary and toolbar actions — the most-used workhorse in the production apps. |
| `ghost` | Low-emphasis actions in dense UI: toolbars, menus, icon buttons. |
| `link` | An action that must sit inline in running text. Rarely needed. |
| `destructive` | Deletion or removal — irreversible or dangerous actions. |
| `warning` | An action that needs caution but isn't destructive. |
| `info` | A neutral, informational highlighted action. |
| `default` | The bare `btn` base, for when you supply your own treatment. |

## Options

| Param | Values | Notes |
| ----- | ------ | ----- |
| `variant` | see **Variants** above | maps to basecoat `btn-*` |
| `size` | `default` `sm` `lg` `icon` | `icon` is the square icon-only size |
| `type` | `button` (default) `submit` `reset` | |
| `loading` | boolean | spinner + disabled while in flight |
| `disabled` | boolean | |
| `classes` | string | extra CSS classes |

## Loading state

`loading` is owned by a small Stimulus controller (`button`). The
label stays in the DOM beside the spinner the whole time, so the
accessible name never disappears mid-action.

**Starting the spinner — two ways:**

- *On click.* Every button carries `data-action="button#load"`, so
  clicking sets the loading value, reveals the spinner and disables the
  button in one step. This is the common case: a submit button that
  must not fire twice.
- *Pre-loaded.* Render with `loading: true` to draw the button already
  spinning and disabled — e.g. a row re-rendered while its operation is
  still in flight.

**Stopping the spinner.** The controller has no stop method by design.
The loading state ends when the button is re-rendered without
`loading:` — and in a normal Turbo flow that happens for free: the
action's response (a redirect, Turbo Stream, frame or morph) replaces
the button with a fresh, non-loading one. To clear it *without*
re-rendering, set the element's `data-button-loading-value` back to
`false`; `loadingValueChanged` then hides the spinner and re-enables the
button.

**When to use it.** Reach for `loading` on actions that make a server
round-trip and must not be triggered twice — form submits and "running"
operations. It fits the request → response → replace lifecycle: the
click starts the spinner, the response clears it. Don't use it for
instant client-side actions (there is nothing to wait for), and don't
leave a button spinning on an action that never re-renders it — let
Turbo replace the button, or reset the value yourself.

## Do and don't

- Do keep one `primary` button per view; demote the rest to
  `outline` or `ghost`.
- Do use `loading` on submit buttons — it disables and spins,
  preventing double submission.
- Do pair icon-only buttons (`size: "icon"`) with an `aria-label`
  via `classes` and a familiar icon.
- Don't disable a button to explain anything — if the action is
  unavailable, say why next to it or omit it. `disabled` is for
  momentary states (in-flight, incomplete form).
- Don't use `destructive` for emphasis; red means deletion.

## Accessibility and content

- Renders a real `<button>` with an explicit `type` — never a styled
  `<div>`; keyboard and form semantics come free.
- The loading state keeps the label in the DOM beside the spinner,
  so the accessible name never disappears mid-action.
- Labels are verb-first, sentence case, naming the outcome ("Add to
  episode") — see the *Content* foundations page.
